# Sudoers rule for test/lab/Invoke-HostAddressChurn.ps1.
#
# Install (as root), then verify:
#   sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root test/lab/yuruna-churn.sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/yuruna-churn
#   sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/yuruna-churn
#
# Why this exists at all: the churn injector forces the host's bridge to take a
# fresh DHCP lease so a cycle can be tested against a known number of address
# changes rather than against whatever the site router happened to do. That is a
# privileged operation, and the test runner is deliberately not root -- so this
# grants the one command it needs and nothing else.
#
# Why the commands are spelled out rather than given as `nmcli *`: a wildcard
# would also permit `nmcli connection modify`, `nmcli connection delete` and
# `nmcli device disconnect`, any of which can take this host off the network
# permanently rather than for the second a renewal costs. The two entries below
# are the version probe the injector uses to check its own privilege, and the
# activation itself, on one named connection.
#
# Replace ytest with the account the runner runs as, and yuruna-br0 if this
# host's bridge is named differently.
ytest ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/nmcli --version
ytest ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/nmcli connection up yuruna-br0
